<!--
  Two items whose bodies are the attack battery, delivered THROUGH the XML
  parser rather than handed straight to the sanitizer.

  This is a genuinely different test from the string battery in the verify
  script: the parser decodes XML entities before the sanitizer ever sees the
  markup, so a payload written as &lt;script&gt; here arrives at the sanitizer as
  a real <script> tag. Anything that reassembles during that decode shows up
  here and nowhere else.

  The title and dc:creator carry payloads too — those go through sanitizeText,
  not sanitizePostHtml.

  ITEM 1 is the scheme/raw-text/CVE battery: payloads whose danger is what they
  ARE.

  ITEM 2 is the SHAPE battery, and it exists because of a hole this fixture
  missed. The first version of this file carried no <picture>/<source> at all,
  while the tag census taken from 52 real Substack bodies counts picture 84 and
  source 84 — the single most common image wrapper in the corpus was the one the
  attack fixture never exercised, and a relative `srcset` on a <source> shipped
  unchecked because of it. So item 2 is built from the census rather than from
  imagination, covering the wrappers real posts actually use and the ones this
  file previously ignored:

    picture/source (84/84)  figure/figcaption (177/30)  div (562)  span (500)
    button + svg + path + polyline (116 each, Substack's image-zoom chrome)
    img with srcset/sizes/data-attrs/fetchpriority/loading (178)
    a with contenteditable/data-component-name/target/rel (1136)
    iframe with a real src (15)  blockquote (69)  ul/li (21/111)  h1  hr (27)

  Nothing in item 2 has a scheme to fail on: every hostile URL in it is
  RELATIVE. That is the point — a scheme check cannot see them.
-->
